  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nWith high pressure over southern Ontario and prominent upper level \nridging over the central CONUS expected to build into the Great \nLakes over the next several days, a tranquil week is ahead. Expect \nlots of sunshine, light winds, and highs generally in the upper 60s \nto low 70s each day. Little to no rainfall is expected over the next \n7 days and mainly dry conditions look to continue through at least \nthe end of the month. We may see some showers out of a cold frontal \npassage early next week but for now chances remain very low (15% or \nless).\n\nDespite some clouds over northwest Ohio and northeast Indiana \novernight with a weak backdoor cold front from Lake Erie, \npatchy to widespread dense fog is possible overnight into \nWednesday morning. Light easterly flow may allow for advection \nfog to develop, especially along and east of I-69. Forecast \nsoundings indicate potential for small dewpoint depressions as \ntemperatures and dewpoints are both expected to be in the low to\nmid 40s tonight. The best timeframe for dense fog will be \nbetween 3 AM to 9 AM EDT Wednesday, with the lowest visibilities\nat or just before daybreak. Current NBM probabilities are about\n20-30% for visibilities less than 3 miles while HREf guidance \nsuggests visibilities may go down to less than 1 mile especially\nin NW Ohio; given that there are still uncertainties and that \nfog can be quite fickle to forecast, future shifts may need to \nhoist an SPS or even a Dense Fog Advisory depending on how low \nvisibilities drop.",
